In CSS, both "font" and "font-family" properties are used to specify the font to be used for text, but they work in slightly different ways:

* "font" property: The "font" property is used to specify the font properties of text, such as font size, font weight, and font style. When you use the "font" property, you can specify individual properties of the font, separated by commas. For example:
```css
font-size: 16px;
font-weight: bold;
font-style: italic;
```
* "font-family" property: The "font-family" property is used to specify the family of fonts to be used for text. When you use the "font-family" property, you can specify a single font family name or a list of font family names, separated by commas. For example:
```css
font-family: 'Open Sans', sans-serif;
```
In this example, the "Open Sans" font family is used for the text. If you specify multiple font families, the browser will use the first one it can render, and fall back to other fonts if necessary.

In summary, the "font" property is used to specify individual font properties, while the "font-family" property is used to specify the family of fonts to be used for text.

